edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186554148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554525 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e4af18f8-8206-55c1-b168-26e7b4b9d10e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554528 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554528 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554528 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554528 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554528 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554528 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554528 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554528 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186554178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554528 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ea82b50a-93b2-5cb1-8254-45300689e485" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554537 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554537 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554537 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554537 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554537 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554537 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554537 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554537 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186554169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554537 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fcc2d5ce-3092-541f-9a44-9ec3c17048dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554539 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554539 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98016" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554539 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554539 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0b/0b2dd71389af5f66bc7931819038922728c3533f89d0de9234514ee24a9d33a5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(-x)-3?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554539 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "044825ff-2a34-548f-8e31-b6e40bc76ccd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554539 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(-x)-3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Reflect it across the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(-x).$\n\n![](images/e2/e2b35429545d9e5c8b4e9217908b4c3755790425ff87cfb699da7aae47f6dc6b.png)\n\n- Then, translate the result down by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f(-x)-3.$\n\n![](images/1b/1b81c0a3c72fa80d35d5dccb462afeb2ba3f1b05c10f009f300fdfd170ed9e76.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554543 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554543 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554543 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5d/5dca91f7173c10e2f274d8611e1308445c4acc72f48595880045143a68d532b1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(-x)+2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1714eddf-429e-5900-b54a-c409f35eac75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554543 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(-x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Reflect it across the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(-x).$\n\n![](images/1b/1b60eeb882c85423bb0550c2b6def0d073d53747479bf322976db313ffdf4109.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, translate the result  up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=f(-x)+2.$\n\n![](images/58/58a681bf9aa47521b30761e904dad8e816e2077387c28b132d2480bb39ea4baa.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554548 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554548 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97793" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554548 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554548 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=-3f(4x),$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Reflection across the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Reflection across the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch of scale factor $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f3c3035-69e9-5d03-a0fc-4433cacd4260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554548 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=-3f(4x),$ we could apply the following steps: \n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3):$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(4x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4):$ Stretch $y=f(4x)$ by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=3f(4x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2):$ Reflect $y=3f(4x)$ across the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=-3f(4x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554553 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554553 #edb/ref 1069 "q-17782" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554553 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554553 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ef/efc577f386353b4120dec733e5e6a627ad2412744dce4d71deba2b500a1edec2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{1}{2}x-1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554553 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "269fb630-a40e-5592-b4e3-251a076c8d27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554553 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=-f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2))\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=-f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x).$\n\n![](images/28/2891d009bd6f5d026ca47afe90f3ec4d6fafc654b7212392b325f76fe43f806e.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, translate the result by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)).$\n\n![](images/ab/ab1f210741e01139618dc2fa1347cf1591cb90ca4f67906b89ebc7df29d77f32.png)\n\n- Finally, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)).$\n\n![](images/e5/e5552a14dc62407e02bb8f5e633ae194a95d313bce04abb3186b56ce83ec2974.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554557 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554557 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554557 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554557 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5b/5b3e36d9b39fcc8d61fe30219d2f3191a1b305a44995b27d5daddb5f18926e02.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-f(x-2)-4?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554557 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b3b851a-20c6-5158-b86d-a090b5acc8fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554557 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x-2)-4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=f(x-2).$\n\n![](images/48/482f1dc55995a299b21894c0ec191c0f8fb8213038cdc5f80da1e77a0d64af4e.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(x-2).$\n\n![](images/a2/a2832ebdf82e2a7bfddccf24fd598758e1ce86fc0726f9c128503574b09fcd0b.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it down by $4$ units to get the graph of $y=-f(x-2)-4.$\n\n![](images/94/9473b7ebfbe7db3d1cfefcf3f7b616ffec4b9ad2e685a6fc40f4560e8d2a1c59.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554558 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554558 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554558 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554558 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=f(-2x)-1,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Reflection across the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Translation by $1$ unit down\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Reflection across the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554558 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b4b8cea-e134-5e94-b33a-7dc117bff583" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554558 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=f(-2x)-1,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4):$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3):$ Reflect $y=f(2x)$ across the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2(-x))=f(-2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2):$ Translate $y=f(-2x)$ down by $1$ unit to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(-2x)-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554580 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554580 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97940" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554580 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554580 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e5/e5138b92d20572b1680dfc010d6e0f8729de9c21c62448cdcb472efbf762c4d1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-f(-2x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554580 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "689e7bfc-91b5-5578-9523-c9029e296a11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554580 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=f(-(2x))\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=-f(-(2x)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(2x).$\n\n![](images/7d/7d76ce80b4212d0cb6cd8b64c424492dd46cad3819ce3cba17bab22c4df115f5.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(-2x).$\n\n![](images/de/ded37839584d99725ab44ebfb4e60e05ea162da6a3845817aa96f6904d67146a.png)\n\n- Finally, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(-2x).$\n\n![](images/6f/6f16d9eed1ed1b5fa41c46124f775238ecb759d79261cc66073c1fb304deb468.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554607 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554607 #edb/ref 1069 "q-17696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554607 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554607 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/50/504024983dafee0fd49c0bb8227f33cc855e2ee9e4bb797f067f0a5e8e415815.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-4f(x-1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554607 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "95bf6a06-ae4e-5d38-8c20-f91522b5fa5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554607 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-4f(x-1),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Translate it by $1$ unit to the right to get the graph of $y=f(x-1).$\n\n![](images/d0/d07f918fae59166e137e47a2c654dfe77ce1c56bb71e931d2d627c509682823e.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=4f(x-1).$\n\n![](images/4c/4c237eeb7ecb85ef71c86b73cdbeaae213e74a21604e079f0df4c14c381f6bd7.png)\n\n  \n\n- Finally, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-4f(x-1).$\n\n![](images/58/583f01b214d4c86dcb00936e83f1fad0a4f0f8901b321880d904b2f0da95d7e8.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554611 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554611 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97783" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554611 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554611 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=f(-(x+5))$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Reflection across the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Translation by $5$ units to the left\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Stretch of scale factor $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Reflection across the $y$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554611 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0d0470d-b24d-5636-b2cb-b9c005b7e95d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554611 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=f(-(x+5)),$ we can apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4):$ Reflect $y=f(x)$ across the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(-x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2):$ Translate $y=f(-x)$ to the left by $5$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(-(x+5)).\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554624 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554624 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115013" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554624 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554624 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/85/8536196127d1e8fc92cd2d442005a386b2d981a027a497ecce363559f52ac0e6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554624 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8c07848-a1dd-5eb7-8320-0b4777fcd645" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554624 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{5}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{5}{2}f(x).$\n\n![](images/ba/bafacd18cae6114ad22fb59d905a8ebb68cc8768a5b20a4a5a2e36f59c11ad11.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{2}f(x).$\n\n![](images/bd/bd69db18de3d58e6806b7ee3473a0fd4e8b6df384cf371be4e9648cac53e3927.png)\n\n- Finally, translate the result down by $4$ units to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4.$\n\n![](images/fe/fe2e7245978e120a538cc87a31d6180c4e342fc438240be1f3ce367eed64bb33.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554643 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554643 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554643 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554643 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/93/938babc27340209a1a5477fb3184abbf155f409c520c76995c4e234f99691347.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{x}{3})?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554643 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f73ff2c0-b98a-5833-8e43-d855656798f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554643 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-f(\\frac{x}{3}),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=3$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{x}{3}).$\n\n![](images/f0/f0b289d42620a275eeb90d4bdbe265ca92ea4264de70ecc11e5f661a84149628.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{x}{3}).$\n\n![](images/59/597ba19a0c993ea597ecd091df02d97e974364125c79780df04aef365442abb6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
